# ============================================================================
# /insights/ — NEXTGEN DATA, the private leadership dashboard.
#
# WHY NOT /data/, WHICH IS WHAT THE BRIEF SUGGESTED
#
# public/data/ is not a free directory. It is the third and last candidate in
# ngs_data_dir() (api/_lib.php:45-64): if the host ever stops letting the code write
# above the web root, the LIVE VOTING DATABASE is created in there, which is why
# .gitignore carries "public/data/*.sqlite" and why that folder answers "Require all
# denied" to everything.
#
# On this host that fallback is not in use — the live data folder resolves to
# /home/.../nextgensummit.us/nextgen-data, outside public_html, which is the only reason
# the votes have survived the deploys (a Hostinger deploy replaces the web root, so a
# database inside it would have been destroyed by the first release after it was created).
#
# But "not in use today" is a fact about a configuration, not a guarantee, and three
# things would have gone wrong had the dashboard been served from there:
#
#   1. Serving a web app out of the directory the database may fall back into means the
#      only thing between the public and the ballot is a FilesMatch rule.
#   2. ngs_protect_dir() (api/_lib.php:34-42) RE-CREATES a deny-all .htaccess and an empty
#      index.html in that directory whenever either is missing. The voting code would
#      periodically seal the dashboard shut.
#   3. That same empty index.html already existed, and DirectoryIndex would serve it
#      ahead of index.php — a blank page where the dashboard should be.
#
# So public/data/ has been left exactly as it was, sealed, and the dashboard lives here.
# Nothing about the voting system was changed to make room for it.
#
# WHAT SECURES THIS DIRECTORY
#
# Not this file. index.php, report.php and export.php each authenticate in PHP before
# emitting a byte, and the session cookie is scoped to /insights/ so no request to any
# other part of the site can carry one. If this .htaccess vanished entirely the dashboard
# would still demand a password. The rules below are belt, not braces.
# ============================================================================

# 1. No directory listing, whatever ends up in here.
Options -Indexes

# 2. Nothing but the three entry points is servable. A stray .json cache, an editor
#    backup or a half-written export cannot be fetched.
<FilesMatch "\.(sqlite|sqlite3|sqlite-journal|sqlite-wal|sqlite-shm|db|jsonl|json|log|bak|tmp|orig|save|swp)$">
  <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
  </IfModule>
  <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order allow,deny
    Deny from all
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

# 3. The headers PHP already sends, repeated here so they still apply if PHP were ever
#    misconfigured into serving these files as plain text.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header always set X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
  Header always set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, private"
</IfModule>

DirectoryIndex index.php
